From c9274695b6342f57fcd0db24a19d085b0bf178c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:08:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): restart-resume selector skips Board lease grants
 released unused [daveey]

Problem: the 2026-11-10 14:23 PST core update restarted the hub gateway under five running cards, and none of
their next runs restored the interrupted conversation (no `restart_resume` event on any, old transcripts intact).
t_01a1a1dea9c64d6a: run 163883 `reclaimed`/owner_restart -> run 164239 `released` {"unused_grant":true} ->
run 164274 started fresh. The other four cards had the same shape (run chains 164178->164241->164304,
164189->164244->164322, 164183->164321->164325, 164317->164318->164319).

Why 1: `restart_candidate` returned None for all five. evidence: read-only replay of the selector over the hub's
rows; gateway/session_kanban_resume.py read only the single immediately preceding task_runs row and required
it to be an owner-restart loss.
Why 2: that row was a grant run, not the restart loss. evidence: run 164239 has only `claimed` (holder/run_uid)
+ `released` {"unused_grant":true} events, no spawned/owner_admitted, no pid, metadata {"unused_grant":true}.
Why 3: the Board grants a lease (writing a run row) before the gateway's final gate; when the gate refuses
(capacity/CPU) or the CAS loses, ClaimGate.__exit__ releases the grant with unused_grant=true
(hermes_cli/kanban_db_sync.py:737-758, kanban_sync_client.py:1994-1997) and the Board's run row syncs back as a
`released` run between the restart loss and the real next run. Every core update restarts the gateway and the
dispatcher then retries claims while the hub is busy, so a grant-then-refuse before the successful claim is
routine, not an edge case.
Root (happened): the selector's "immediately preceding run" rule was written for crashes/timeouts in between
and assumed every run row is an execution; Board grants are run rows that execute nothing.
Root (not caught): tests/gateway/test_session_kanban_restart_resume.py claims on an unsynced board
(`kb.claim_task` direct), which never produces grant rows, so no test covered the primary/synced claim path that
production uses. PR 266 fixed uncounted retries, not preservation, so nothing exercised restoration with grants.
Recurrence: first occurrence of this chain; earlier restoration commits 6c1154108fd and 340f8d69833 built the
resume and did not cover grant rows.

Fix: restart_candidate walks back over rows that are explicitly released unused grants AND carry no execution
or admission evidence (status/outcome released, ended, metadata exactly {"unused_grant": true}, no
error/summary/worker_pid/heartbeat/claim_lock, events limited to the Board's claimed (no local `lock`) and a
released with unused_grant true), at most 32, then applies the unchanged owner-restart test to the first other
row. Crash, timeout, completion, ordinary release, a live grant, or any row with execution evidence still blocks.
The `restart_resume` event records `skipped_unused_grant_runs`.

Proof: tests/gateway/test_session_kanban_restart_resume_unused_grant.py (45 tests) builds the rows through the
real path: HubLeaseClient against the Board fake (release now carries unused_grant like the Board), claim_gate +
ClaimGate release, and ksc.sync_board pull/apply into the local DB, then the real run_task admission. Covers
1 and 3 grants restoring the exact transcript and emitting the event, pending tool call, compaction,
foreign home/profile fallbacks, new-run ownership, and 40+ blocking cases (each condition mutated by exact
anchor). Without the fix 35 of the 45 fail.
